{"qid":"Q236","names":["Montenegro"],"title":"Montenegro","endonym":{"native":"Crna Gora","lang":"cnr"},"capsule":"Montenegro (Crna Gora, 'Black Mountain') consolidated as a distinct principality under the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty from the early eighteenth century, surviving Ottoman encroachment as a de facto autonomous mountain polity centred on Cetinje. It gained formal recognition of independence at the Congress of Berlin in 1878, which also awarded significant territorial enlargements; Nikola I proclaimed it a kingdom in 1910. Montenegro was occupied by Austria-Hungary in World War I and merged into the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes in 1918; it declared independence from the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro on 3 June 2006 and acceded to NATO in 2017.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Montenegro's current boundaries are precisely defined; the attestation window from 1853 captures the principality under Danilo I Petrović's secularising reforms and extends to the present day.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Montenegro","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Montenegro"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Montenegro","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montenegro"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Petrović-Njegoš dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrovi%C4%87-Njego%C5%A1_dynasty"}],"britannica":"place/Montenegro","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Montenegro","rulers":[{"qid":"Q9178502","name":"Božo Petrović-Njegoš","start":1879,"end":1905,"image":"Bozo Petrovic-Njegos portrait.tif","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bozo%20Petrovic-Njegos%20portrait.tif?width=320","